## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design multi-cloud infrastructure with consistent module boundaries, robust state management, and policy-driven gates, reflecting real-world enterprise needs.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliases and multiple backends
- Shared module design for multi-cloud resources
- Per-environment state isolation and plan gating
- Drift Detection and policy enforcement (OPA/Sentinel)
- CI/CD integration for plan-only gates

## Code Example

```hcl
# Minimal sketch illustrating two providers with aliases and a multi-env config
provider "aws" {
  region = var.aws_region
  alias  = "aws_prod"
}
provider "google" {
  project = var.gcp_project
  region  = var.gcp_region
  alias   = "gcp_prod"
}

module "messaging" {
  source = "./modules/messaging"
  config = {
    prod = {
      aws  = { region = "us-east-1" }
      gcp  = { project = "prod-project" }
    }
    staging = {
      aws  = { region = "us-west-2" }
      gcp  = { project = "staging-project" }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle secrets rotation across clouds in Terraform?
- What are the trade-offs of using Sentinel vs OPA for this gating scenario?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cloud multi-tenant infra from a single module, balancing reuse with isolation. Targets provider aliasing, per-tenant tagging, and policy-driven gates.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliasing and for_each
- Cross-cloud module composition
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Vault) for prod changes
- CI gates around per-tenant plan validation

## Code Example

```hcl
# skeleton: tenants map and module per-tenant
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({
    cloud  = string
    region = string
  }))
}

module "tenant" {
  for_each = var.tenants
  source   = "./modules/tenant"

  tenant_name = each.key
  cloud       = each.value.cloud
  region      = each.value.region

  # pass per-tenant providers (pseudo-syntax for illustration)
  providers = {
    aws    = aws.${each.value.cloud == "aws" ? "alias" : "default"}
    google = google.${each.value.cloud == "gcp" ? "alias" : "default"}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test drift with cross-cloud resources and ensure idempotence? 
- What are trade-offs between centralized tenants state vs per-tenant backends?
